  "summary": "A type of low-cost, prefabricated concrete panel housing constructed during the Nikita Khrushchev era to address urban housing shortages.",
  "description": "Characterized by small apartments, standardized layouts, and repetitive facades, these buildings prioritized quantity over quality. They represented Soviet efforts to rapidly improve living conditions despite economic constraints and technological limitations.",
  "culturalContext": "The khrushchyovka — Nikita Khrushchev's prefab five-story concrete apartment blocks (1957-1964) — housed millions moving into Soviet cities, ending communal flats with standardized one-room-to-three-room units at socialist cost: low ceilings, tiny kitchens (\"Khrushchev was determined to cook on two burners\"), walk-up stairs, and panel joints that whistled in wind. Maligned then, they are now heritage-adjacent — photogenic in golden-hour panels, studied as the largest housing experiment ever, and still home to tens of millions across the former USSR.",
